WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.506
Concurrent data structures appear anywhere multiple threads, tasks, or asynchronous workers share access to state.

2
00:00:05.506 --> 00:00:12.479
They may look like ordinary containers, queues, maps, caches, registries, pools, or tables, but the operating conditions are different.

3
00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:19.453
In single-threaded code, a caller can often assume that a container stays unchanged while an operation is running.

4
00:00:19.453 --> 00:00:26.427
Under concurrency, another thread may be reading, inserting, erasing, shutting down, or replacing related state at the same time.

5
00:00:26.427 --> 00:00:31.565
That change matters because a data structure is more than memory that holds values.

6
00:00:31.565 --> 00:00:37.071
It also has invariants: relationships that must remain true for the structure to be correct.

7
00:00:37.071 --> 00:00:41.476
A queue may require its size counter to match the stored items.

8
00:00:41.476 --> 00:00:46.247
A cache may require its index, eviction list, and lifetime policy to agree.

9
00:00:46.247 --> 00:00:52.487
A registry may require that a name, handle, and object remain linked until all users are done.

10
00:00:52.487 --> 00:00:59.828
If different threads update those pieces without a shared rule, the program can drift into states that no caller expected.

11
00:00:59.828 --> 00:01:03.131
Defensive C++ engineering treats these structures as explicit boundaries.

12
00:01:03.131 --> 00:01:11.573
The design should say who owns the state, which operations are safe concurrently, which operations require exclusive access, and when objects may be

13
00:01:11.573 --> 00:01:11.940
destroyed.

14
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.509
This is not only a performance topic.

15
00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:20.749
Broken synchronization can cause reliability failures, data corruption, incorrect authorization decisions, resource exhaustion, and hard-to-repeat

16
00:01:20.749 --> 00:01:21.850
crashes during shutdown.

17
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:30.292
The goal of this course is to build a practical roadmap: protect invariants, manage lifetimes, prefer clear APIs, and verify the design with

18
00:01:30.292 --> 00:01:31.760
diagnostics, tests, and review.
